okay hello everyone my name is Nicolas I  work at front of Institute for solar  energy and I want to tell you what we do  with satellite images this is me and a  colleague standing next to our satellite  dish with this dish we receive satellite  images from a geostationary satellite  this is what a satellite image looks  like we receive oh yeah I hope you can  see it every receive bottom that was  every 15 minutes all the time in real  time and with this image we compute  first we compute something called in a  pitot it's hard to see but it's  basically what earth would look like if  there were no clouds with this thing we  can then compute something called a  cloud index that basically tells you how  much clouds are there in a given  location if it's bright there's a cloud  if it's dark there's no cloud next we  use something called a clear sky model a  clear sky model tells you for a given  location and time what the solar  irradiance at that location in time  would be if there were no clouds solar  irradiance is measured in watts per  square meter in this clear sky model  combined with the cloud enix  gives us a time series of what the solar  irradiance was at the location this  method is cool what we can do is we can  take actual solar irradiance  measurements from ground sensors and  then we can fit our model parameters so  that our satellite method fits the  ground measurements but this method only  tells me what the irradiance is now or  what it was in the past I also want to  know what it's in the future so what we  do is we take those cloud indices and we  compute something called a cloud motion  vector basically that's an optical flow  it tells me how the clouds moved in the  past  and if I assume that the cloud movement  will stay the same I can actually do use  that to predict how the cloud indices  will look like in the future and with a  lot the next forecasts I can then create  a solar irradiance forecast  [Music]  next let's assume you have a PV power  plant if I know enough about your PV  power plant I can put in irradiance data  and create something clean create  simulated PV power like basically I  simulate your power plant and again what  I can do is if I have measurements power  measurements from your plant I can  fine-tune the plant simulation  parameters so that my simulated PV power  fits your power measurements and once I  have the optimized simulation I can put  in my irradiance forecast and create a  PV power forecast and with that I  conclude what we do with satellite  images thanks  [Applause]
 

